import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Measuring a GUI app without lying to yourself — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="You cannot profile a desktop app by using it, because you never do the same thing twice. Here is the harness I built instead, and the three reports that changed what I worked on."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-15">September 15, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Measuring a GUI app without lying to yourself</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          You cannot profile a desktop app by clicking around in it, because you
          never do the same thing twice. Here is the harness I built instead,
          and the three reports that changed what I worked on.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 5 min read</p>
      </header>

      <p>
        I spent a good while making the thumbnail grid faster. It was already
        fast. Nobody was waiting on it.
      </p>
      <p>
        What people were actually waiting on was opening a RAW file, which I had
        never measured, because measuring it properly was annoying and I had a
        feeling about where the time was going.
      </p>
      <p>The feeling was wrong.</p>

      <h2>Why you cannot just use the app</h2>
      <p>
        Attach a profiler, click around for thirty seconds, read the flame
        graph. That works for finding something catastrophic. It does not work
        for deciding whether a change helped, which is the question I actually
        care about.
      </p>
      <p>
        The problem is that you cannot do the same thing twice. On run one you
        scrolled a bit fast and reached row 40. On run two, row 31. On run one
        the thumbnail cache was empty, and on run two it was full, because run
        one filled it.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 186" role="img" aria-label="Dot plot: six manual runs scatter widely across the time axis, while six scripted profile runs land on nearly the same value.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">SIX RUNS OF THE SAME &ldquo;BENCHMARK&rdquo;</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="52">Clicking around</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="70">in the app</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="176" y1="56" x2="624" y2="56"></line>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="192" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="268" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="341" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="433" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="521" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" cx="598" cy="56" r="6"></circle>
          <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" d="M192 76 V84 M192 80 H598 M598 76 V84"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="395" y="102" text-anchor="middle">the spread is your own hands</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="136">--profile</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="154">the same script, always</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="176" y1="140" x2="624" y2="140"></line>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="392" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="399" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="404" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="397" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="401" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bar" cx="395" cy="140" r="6"></circle>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="398" y="172" text-anchor="middle">a change you can actually read</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Your before number and your after number describe different sessions.
          You can squint at that and convince yourself of anything, which is
          exactly what I had been doing.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Run the code, not the app</h2>
      <p>
        My fix was a flag. <code>--profile &lt;folder&gt;</code> starts the app,
        runs the real folder listing, the real loader, the real catalog and the
        real thumbnail code against that folder, prints timings and exits. No
        window is opened. The UI library and the graphics card are never
        involved.
      </p>
      <p>
        What it runs is a script, not a simulation. It lists the folder, fills
        thumbnails for 120 photos, which is about two screenfuls, then opens ten
        photos in sequence, the way you do when stepping through a shoot with
        the arrow keys. Those numbers sit in the code with a comment explaining
        each one. A workload is a claim about how people use the product, so the
        claim belongs next to the number it produces.
      </p>
      <p>
        The whole harness is about 230 lines. It is not sophisticated. It just
        produces the same number twice.
      </p>

      <h2>Keeping it out of the shipped build</h2>
      <p>
        The instrumentation is a set of attributes on functions, behind two
        Cargo features that are off by default. With the features off, the macro
        hands the function body back unchanged, so the release build carries
        nothing and the browser build's size does not move.
      </p>
      <p>
        One wrinkle caught me out. The profiler has to be a normal dependency
        rather than an optional one, because the attribute has to resolve in
        every build even when it expands to nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>The first report</h2>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 196" role="img" aria-label="Bar chart: listing a folder takes 3 to 5 milliseconds, filling 120 warm thumbnails 22 milliseconds, a RAW preview 11 milliseconds, and a sharp RAW decode 170 to 210 milliseconds.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">300 PHOTOS, WARM CACHE</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="51">List the folder</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="34" width="9" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="245" y="51">3 to 5 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="91">Fill 120 thumbnails</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="74" width="46" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="282" y="91">22 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="131">Open a RAW, preview</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="226" y="114" width="23" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="259" y="131">11 ms</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="0" y="171">Open a RAW, sharp</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="226" y="154" width="400" height="24" rx="2"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-on-bar" x="238" y="171">170 to 210 ms &middot; all of the wait is here</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Most of this chart means "stop working on this".
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        I had been optimising the wrong thing, and the report said so in about
        four seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        It also pointed at a fix I would never have found by staring at code.
        The app pre-fetches the cheap preview for the next photo, but not the
        expensive sharp decode. So arrowing through a folder of RAW files pays
        the 200 ms every single time, even though the app knew half a second in
        advance which photo you were about to look at.
      </p>

      <h2>The second report</h2>
      <p>
        The profiler can count bytes allocated per function instead of time. One
        function was responsible for 12.0 MB out of 25.3 MB, 47% of everything
        the app allocated in that session. It was the function that hands
        thumbnails to the UI library for drawing.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cause was an API shape, not an algorithm. The UI library's image
        type can only be built by copying pixels into a fresh buffer, and the
        pixels were already sitting in the loader's cache in exactly the right
        layout. Writing them straight into a GPU texture took that function from
        12.0 MB to 0.16 MB, and the whole session from 25.3 MB to 13.4 MB.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing was slow, and no user would ever have filed a bug about it. But
        a WebAssembly heap never gives memory back once it has grown, so in the
        browser build a temporary allocation is not temporary. It permanently
        raises the tab's footprint.
      </p>

      <h2>The third report</h2>
      <p>
        On 200 photos with a warm cache, decoding all 200 thumbnails across
        8 workers took 39.5 ms in total. Analysing one photo took 7.39 ms, so
        1.48 seconds for the batch. Analysis runs on the main thread and cannot
        be spread across cores, so it costs about fifty-five times what the
        decoding costs.
      </p>
      <p>
        My plan had been to make the decoding faster and fetch it all in
        advance. Both would have been worthless.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>A benchmark's job is to be repeatable, not realistic.</strong>
          Realistic and unrepeatable tells you nothing, because there is nothing
          to compare it to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cut the human out of the loop.</strong> If measuring your
          system needs someone driving it, script the sequence instead, even if
          the script is less faithful than a real session.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the workload's shape next to the code.</strong> "120
          thumbnails, because that is two screenfuls" is a claim someone can
          disagree with. "120" on its own is a magic number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Measure more than one axis.</strong> The 47% allocation
          finding was invisible in every timing report I had, and it mattered
          most on the platform with the tightest constraints.
        </li>
      </ul>



